    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d653">

                <pb facs="0976" n="856" xml:id="pg856"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.74/246</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The President of the Federal Telegraph Company</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Schwerin</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                            State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >San Francisco</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-08-29"><hi rendition="#italic">August 29,
                        1922</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received August 31 (?)]</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to advise concerning the
                    contract between the Chinese Government and the Federal Telegraph Company of
                    California that the Federal Telegraph Company of California and the Radio
                    Corporation of America have entered into an engagement to finance and construct
                    the several radio stations in China through the formation of a new company
                    called the Federal Telegraph Company of Delaware which will be owned and
                    controlled by the Federal Telegraph Company of California and the Radio
                    Corporation of America, jointly.</p>
                <p>All the original conditions of the Chinese contract are to be fully performed by
                    the Federal Telegraph Company of Delaware.</p>
                <p>I submit herewith copies in duplicate,<anchor n="18" xml:id="d653-f1"/> numbers
                    1, 2 and 3, setting forth all the understandings between the Federal Telegraph
                    Company of California and the Radio Corporation of America, with the earnest
                    hope that the Department has no objection to the arrangements which have been
                    tentatively made by the companies herein mentioned and that the Department will
                    instruct the American Minister at Peking to extend to me all appropriate
                    diplomatic support in my negotiations with the Chinese Government in obtaining
                    the acceptance by that Government of the arrangements which I have made and
                    which are set forth in the pamphlets herewith enclosed.</p>
                <p>Very respectfully,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">R. P. Schwerin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
